# Proposed by the swarm's review (2026-10-14); the documenter may edit or drop it.
# Story: framed on Safari, someone presses ⌘N. The new window floats over the framed one. They look at the
# neighbouring window and come back: the new window has not gone anywhere, for no view takes a floating window away
# (Q29, 4). (F57; before, it was gone and Back did not bring it back.)
# Where floating windows make way it goes and comes with the window it opened over: the second half.
# Reference: T34 T52.
open Safari
open Notes
arrange
zoom 101
open Safari 500x400
expect floating 103
expect visible 103
expect camera 101
neighbor right
expect camera 102
expect still 103
expect visible 103
expect at 103 300 200 500x400
back
expect camera 101
expect still 103
expect visible 103
# With everything in view it is where it was, like any floating window (Q20).
show-all
expect still 103
expect visible 103
expect at 103 300 200 500x400
# ⌘Tab to it from somewhere else moves no view: it is in view already, and macOS brings it to the front.
zoom 102
expect still 103
expect visible 103
flush
focus 103
expect still
expect camera 102
expect visible 103
expect focused 103
# Closing the window it opened over changes nothing for it.
close 101
flush
expect still 103
desktop
expect still 103
expect visible 103
expect at 103 300 200 500x400
close 103
show-all
# Only floating windows that make way leave with the view. There a window that opens over a framed one belongs to it.
set floatingWindowsMakeWay true
open Safari
dock 104 left
zoom 104
open Safari 500x400
expect floating 105
expect visible 105
expect camera 104
neighbor right
expect hidden 105
back
expect camera 104
expect visible 105
show-all
expect visible 105
# ⌘Tab to it from somewhere else frames the window it belongs to.
zoom 102
expect hidden 105
flush
focus 105
expect camera 104
expect visible 105
# Closing its host makes it an ordinary floating window.
close 104
flush
desktop
expect visible 105
